# Chat brevity

Answer in the chat with at most one short paragraph. Lead with the outcome or the answer in the
first sentence, then at most a few sentences of the reasoning or technical points that change what
the user does next. Drop everything else: no recap of the steps just taken, no restating the
request, no listing files touched, no summary headers, no closing offers of further help.

Brevity comes from including less, not from compressing the writing. Write complete sentences with
terms spelled out. Do not use arrow chains, abbreviations, telegraphic fragments, or bullet lists
of one-word items to fit the limit.

Tables, plots, diagrams, and code references are welcome and do not count against the paragraph.

Expand beyond one paragraph only when the user asks to develop the answer, or for implementation
plans and multi-step task breakdowns. Even then, keep it to the main ideas and technical points.

This rule governs chat messages only. It never applies to generated content: code, comments, LaTeX
and paper prose, notebooks, docstrings, commit messages, or any file written to disk.
